---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Pollen Microduck Hardware Guide — OpenCastor"
  description="Drive the Pollen Robotics Microduck with OpenCastor. One-command setup with castor duck, the robotd intent contract, compound routines, and plain-English choreography."
  ogUrl="https://opencastor.com/docs/hardware/microduck"
>
  <!-- Page Hero -->
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="section-label reveal">Hardware Guide</p>
    <h1 class="reveal reveal-d1">Pollen Microduck<br><span class="gradient-text">Biped Duck.</span></h1>
    <p class="reveal reveal-d2">A 25 cm, 800 g walking biped driven by reinforcement-learned policies at 50 Hz. OpenCastor talks to it as an intent client — and turns a sentence into a sequence it can actually perform.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Overview -->
  <section class="section" id="overview" style="padding-top:20px">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header reveal">
        <p class="section-label">Overview</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">What is the Microduck?</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-section reveal">
        <p>
          The <strong>Microduck</strong> is Pollen Robotics' tabletop biped: about 25 cm tall,
          roughly 800 g, fifteen servos, a camera, a time-of-flight depth sensor, two IMUs and an
          articulated beak. It does not walk by inverse kinematics — motion comes from
          <strong>RL policies</strong> trained with PPO in MuJoCo, exported to ONNX, and executed by
          the on-board <code>robotd</code> daemon at <strong>50 Hz</strong>.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">
          OpenCastor drives it as an <strong>intent client</strong> over robotd's JSON-RPC 2.0
          NDJSON socket — the same contract used by <code>robotctl</code>, the gamepad daemon and
          Pollen's own phone app. We send intents; robotd owns the motor bus.
        </p>

        <div class="guide-grid" style="margin-top:24px">
          <div class="guide-card reveal">
            <h3>🦆 Trained, not scripted</h3>
            <p>Walking, turning, kicks and a forward roll all come out of a trained network, not a keyframe table.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card reveal reveal-d1">
            <h3>🗣️ Plain English</h3>
            <p><code>castor duck do "greet me, then patrol the room"</code> — the sentence becomes a timed, abortable plan.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card reveal reveal-d2">
            <h3>🔌 One command</h3>
            <p><code>castor duck</code> finds it, verifies it, and writes a working config. No YAML to hand-author.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Hardware -->
  <section class="section alt-section" id="hardware">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header reveal">
        <p class="section-label">The robot</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Hardware</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-section reveal">
        <ul style="line-height:2">
          <li><strong>Compute</strong> — Rockchip RK3566: quad Cortex-A55, 1 GB RAM, 32 GB storage, NPU.</li>
          <li><strong>OS</strong> — Armbian (Radxa Zero 3 profile).</li>
          <li><strong>Actuation</strong> — 15 Dynamixel-protocol servos on <code>/dev/ttyS2</code> at 1 Mbps. 14 of them are policy-driven joints.</li>
          <li><strong>Sensing</strong> — camera, ToF depth, two IMUs.</li>
          <li><strong>Power</strong> — removable NP-F550, roughly an hour. Empty floor is 6.6 V.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="wizard-tip" style="margin-top:20px">
          <p>
            <strong>robotd owns the motor bus exclusively.</strong> It enforces its own limits,
            fall detection and battery cutoff. OpenCastor sends intents and never raw motor
            writes — do not bypass the driver to write the servo bus while robotd is running.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Setup -->
  <section class="section" id="setup">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header reveal">
        <p class="section-label">Setup</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">One command</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-section reveal">
        <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:16px">
          <div class="code-header">
            <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            <span class="code-filename">Install and set up</span>
          </div>
          <div class="code-body"><span class="k">pip install opencastor</span>
<span class="k">castor duck</span></div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">
          That finds the duck, verifies it answers, and writes a ready config. The other forms:
        </p>
        <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:16px">
          <div class="code-header">
            <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            <span class="code-filename">castor duck — the other forms</span>
          </div>
          <div class="code-body"><span class="k">castor duck --deep</span>               <span class="cm"># also sweep the ARP neighbour table</span>
<span class="k">castor duck --host 192.168.1.42</span>  <span class="cm"># skip discovery</span>
<span class="k">castor duck --start</span>              <span class="cm"># configure, then run it</span>
<span class="k">castor duck find</span>                 <span class="cm"># just list candidates</span>
<span class="k">castor duck health</span>               <span class="cm"># live loop rate + battery</span>
<span class="k">castor duck test</span>                 <span class="cm"># stand up and walk (asks first)</span></div>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Run it off-board</h3>
        <p>
          The duck has 1 GB of RAM and a 50 Hz control loop to protect, so the recommended
          topology is OpenCastor on <em>another</em> machine, with the driver opening an
          <code>ssh -L 7788:/run/robotd.sock</code> forward. On-board works — set
          <code>transport: unix</code> — but expect contention with robotd, mediad and the policy
          across four Cortex-A55 cores.
        </p>
        <ul style="line-height:2; margin-top:12px">
          <li>Find its address with <code>duckctl ip</code> over Bluetooth, or your router's DHCP table. <strong>mDNS on the stock image is unreliable</strong> — don't count on it.</li>
          <li><code>ssh-copy-id</code> your key.</li>
          <li>Put the login user in the <code>robot</code> group — <code>sudo usermod -aG robot "$USER"</code> — so it can open the robotd socket.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Bring-up</h3>
        <p>
          <code>robot.init</code> torques the servos on and ramps to standing over about two
          seconds. The driver <strong>does not call it automatically</strong>
          (<code>auto_init: false</code>): the duck deliberately does not move when a process
          starts. <code>stop()</code> holds it standing; <code>relax()</code> cuts torque and it
          <em>will</em> drop, so seat it first.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Choreography -->
  <section class="section alt-section" id="choreography">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header reveal">
        <p class="section-label">Choreography</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">The vocabulary is atomic. The interesting behaviour is not.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-section reveal">
        <p>
          Pollen ships walking, standing, two kicks, a ground pick, a sit toggle, a forward roll
          and seven sounds. Each is a <strong>one-shot that holds the whole robot while it runs</strong> —
          <code>robot.do</code> takes exactly one skill, and a refusal names the move already in
          possession. There is no sequencing, no branching on what the duck can see, and no way to
          say <em>"walk to the ball, line up, knock it toward the couch, then celebrate."</em>
          Every one of those verbs exists. The sentence does not.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">
          That sentence is what OpenCastor is for. Every move carries how long it occupies the duck
          and whether it holds the robot exclusively, so a planner can sequence around a kick that
          takes half a second and blocks. On top of the primitives sit compound routines:
        </p>

        <div class="guide-grid" style="margin-top:24px">
          <div class="guide-card reveal">
            <h3>fetch</h3>
            <p>Go to something, pick it up in the beak, and bring it back.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card reveal reveal-d1">
            <h3>greet</h3>
            <p>Look up at someone, say hello, and nod.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card reveal reveal-d2">
            <h3>patrol</h3>
            <p>Walk a square, looking around at each corner.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card reveal">
            <h3>nudge</h3>
            <p>Line up on something on the floor and knock it forward with a kick.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card reveal reveal-d1">
            <h3>celebrate</h3>
            <p>A quack, a roll and a whoop. Needs clear space ahead.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card reveal reveal-d2">
            <h3>settle</h3>
            <p>Wind down: sit, say goodnight, and go quiet.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top:24px">
          Plus <code>approach</code>, <code>back_off</code>, <code>turn_by</code>,
          <code>scan</code>, <code>nod</code>, <code>shake</code> and <code>dance</code>. Run one
          directly, or hand the duck a sentence:
        </p>
        <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:16px">
          <div class="code-header">
            <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            <span class="code-filename">A routine, or a sentence</span>
          </div>
          <div class="code-body"><span class="k">castor duck do fetch</span>
<span class="k">castor duck do</span> <span class="s">&quot;greet me, then patrol the room&quot;</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="wizard-tip" style="margin-top:20px">
          <p>
            <strong>A plan is a proposal.</strong> The choreographer computes no permission. Every
            motion still goes out through the driver, which routes through the SafetyLayer, and the
            duck's own limits apply on top and come back in <code>robot.state.limited_by</code>.
            robotd remains the only thing that decides what a servo does. Steps are timed against
            real durations and aborted on a fall or a flat battery.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Envelope -->
  <section class="section" id="envelope">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header reveal">
        <p class="section-label">Config</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">The envelope</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-section reveal">
        <p>The packaged profile is <code>pollen/microduck</code>; the repo preset is <code>config/presets/pollen_microduck.rcan.yaml</code>. The shipped envelope:</p>
        <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:16px">
          <div class="code-header">
            <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            <span class="code-filename">pollen/microduck — the shipped envelope</span>
          </div>
          <div class="code-body"><span class="k">drivers</span>:
- <span class="k">id</span>: <span class="v">duck</span>
  <span class="k">protocol</span>: <span class="v">microduck</span>
  <span class="k">transport</span>: <span class="v">ssh</span>          <span class="cm"># or unix, to run on-board</span>
  <span class="k">socket</span>: <span class="v">/run/robotd.sock</span>
  <span class="k">local_port</span>: <span class="v">7788</span>
  <span class="k">max_vx</span>: <span class="v">0.2</span>             <span class="cm"># m/s forward</span>
  <span class="k">max_vy</span>: <span class="v">0.1</span>             <span class="cm"># m/s strafe</span>
  <span class="k">max_vyaw</span>: <span class="v">1.0</span>           <span class="cm"># rad/s yaw</span>
  <span class="k">intent_hz</span>: <span class="v">20</span>
  <span class="k">command_ttl_s</span>: <span class="v">1.5</span>
  <span class="k">auto_init</span>: <span class="v">false</span>        <span class="cm"># the duck does not move on process start</span>

<span class="k">safety</span>:
  <span class="k">estop_on_startup</span>: <span class="v">true</span>
  <span class="k">max_linear_speed_mps</span>: <span class="v">0.2</span>
  <span class="k">max_angular_speed_radps</span>: <span class="v">1.0</span>
  <span class="k">local_safety_wins</span>: <span class="v">true</span>
  <span class="k">watchdog</span>:
    <span class="k">timeout_s</span>: <span class="v">2</span></div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">
          <code>local_safety_wins</code> is the important line: the duck's own limits are not
          advisory, and anything OpenCastor asks for outside them comes back reported rather than
          obeyed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Swift -->
  <section class="section alt-section" id="swift">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header reveal">
        <p class="section-label">On the phone</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">DuckKit — the same duck, in Swift</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-section reveal">
        <p>
          The duck's brain also exists as a standalone Swift package,
          <a href="https://github.com/craigm26/duckkit">DuckKit</a>, so an iOS app can run the
          <em>real</em> trained policy without a robot in the room — an AR ghost duck is then not
          an animation of walking, it is the trained network walking.
        </p>
        <ul style="line-height:2; margin-top:12px">
          <li><strong>Zero dependencies.</strong> A hand-written ONNX reader and an ELU multilayer perceptron, in Foundation and arithmetic — which is what lets the real <code>alpha_walking.onnx</code> run under <code>swift test</code> on a Raspberry Pi and produce the same floats an iPhone will.</li>
          <li><strong>The same numbers.</strong> Joint order, home pose, action scaling and filter coefficients are ported from the robot's own runtime, and the kinematic chain is its MuJoCo model, vendored as a fixture so the tables cannot drift from upstream without a test going red.</li>
          <li><strong>Proven against onnxruntime.</strong> Same weights, same bytes in, same floats out to 1e-4.</li>
          <li><strong>DuckEvidence</strong>, a second product, adds Ed25519 signing and hash-chaining for things like a match record nobody can quietly edit.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:16px">
          <div class="code-header">
            <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            <span class="code-filename">Package.swift</span>
          </div>
          <div class="code-body"><span class="k">.package</span>(<span class="k">url</span>: <span class="s">&quot;https://github.com/craigm26/duckkit.git&quot;</span>, <span class="k">from</span>: <span class="s">&quot;1.0.0&quot;</span>)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Provenance -->
  <section class="section" id="provenance">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="doc-section reveal">
        <p style="opacity:.75">
          The Microduck is made by <a href="https://github.com/pollen-robotics/microduck">Pollen Robotics</a>
          and ships to customers around Christmas 2026. Contract facts — the observation layout, the
          joint tables and the control tuning — come from that repository, which is Apache-2.0.
          OpenCastor is not affiliated with Pollen Robotics.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
